  "notes": "A literary compound of {困|こま}る (to be troubled) and {苦|く} (suffering). Used in formal writing and speeches, not in everyday conversation. Often appears in historical and biographical narratives describing difficult living conditions.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {困苦|こんく}に{耐|た}える — to endure hardship\n- {困苦|こんく}{欠乏|けつぼう} — hardship and want\n- {困苦|こんく}を{乗|の}り{越|こ}える — to overcome hardship\n- {困苦|こんく}の{生活|せいかつ} — a life of hardship\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {苦労|くろう} — hardship, trouble; the most common everyday word for difficulty and hard work\n- {苦難|くなん} — suffering, ordeal; similar formality level, focuses on painful trials\n- {辛苦|しんく} — toil, hard labor; emphasizes the painful effort involved\n- {難儀|なんぎ} — difficulty, trouble; can also mean troublesome or arduous",
  "schema_version": "2.0",
